525
00:22:26,178 --> 00:22:27,380
- Good idea.

526
00:22:27,380 --> 00:22:28,815
Mickey, you remember you promised me some

527
00:22:28,815 --> 00:22:30,450
of those rose cuttings.

528
00:22:30,450 --> 00:22:32,052
- Nice seeing you again, Mickey.

529
00:22:32,052 --> 00:22:34,087
Going to have to get you down
to the barber shop one day--

530
00:22:34,087 --> 00:22:35,255
- [Mike] I like Mickey.

531
00:22:38,692 --> 00:22:40,927
(clanking)

532
00:22:42,695 --> 00:22:44,865
(beeping)

533
00:22:46,232 --> 00:22:48,468
(slamming)

534
00:22:50,770 --> 00:22:52,138
- Well mother, what should we do

535
00:22:52,138 --> 00:22:53,773
with our naughty miscreants?

536
00:22:53,773 --> 00:22:57,343
- I think we should give them
milk and cookies, hurray!

537
00:22:57,343 --> 00:22:58,178
- Hi guys.

538
00:22:58,178 --> 00:22:59,012
- Oh hi, Mike.

539
00:22:59,012 --> 00:23:00,780
You're just in time to
enter our wonderful,

540
00:23:00,780 --> 00:23:03,015
clay based world of whimsy and wonder.

541
00:23:03,015 --> 00:23:04,885
- Where under the surface
lurks a rat's nest

542
00:23:04,885 --> 00:23:06,587
of seething violence and hatred.

543
00:23:06,587 --> 00:23:07,887
Wee!

544
00:23:07,887 --> 00:23:08,722
- Hurray.

545
00:23:08,722 --> 00:23:09,555
- Oh neat.

546
00:23:09,555 --> 00:23:10,490
You wanna unpack that for me?

547
00:23:10,490 --> 00:23:11,323
- Sure Mike.

548
00:23:11,323 --> 00:23:13,960
Welcome to the land of our
two little clay figures,

549
00:23:13,960 --> 00:23:15,895
Bowless and Horse Fly.

550
00:23:15,895 --> 00:23:16,730
- What do they do?

551
00:23:16,730 --> 00:23:19,365
- Well mostly they gather
lint, hair, and animal dander.

552
00:23:19,365 --> 00:23:22,602
- But they share an
impassioned hatred of robots.

553
00:23:22,602 --> 00:23:24,972
Move the robots up there, will you, Mike?

554
00:23:24,972 --> 00:23:27,207
- In this colorful
story our two pranksters

555
00:23:27,207 --> 00:23:29,608
hire some well meaning unsuspecting robots

556
00:23:29,608 --> 00:23:31,410
who want nothing more
than to serve humanity

557
00:23:31,410 --> 00:23:32,712
to the best of their abilities.

558
00:23:32,712 --> 00:23:34,447
- And the two retched lumps of filth

559
00:23:34,447 --> 00:23:36,717
carry out a campaign
of terror and savagery

560
00:23:36,717 --> 00:23:39,418
on the handsome helpful robots.

561
00:23:39,418 --> 00:23:41,020
- Okay, I think I see where you're going

562
00:23:41,020 --> 00:23:41,855
with this, you guys.

563
00:23:41,855 --> 00:23:43,457
- Good, you work the
lumps, we'll do the voices.

564
00:23:43,457 --> 00:23:45,258
- Oh guys, come on. I know
you're hurting but let's just--

565
00:23:45,258 --> 00:23:48,262
- Please Mike, just work the lumps!

566
00:23:48,262 --> 00:23:50,363
- Okay, alright, fine.

567
00:23:50,363 --> 00:23:52,598
(mumbling)

568
00:23:58,905 --> 00:24:01,373
- Okay now, Mike, make the
lumps smash the robots.

569
00:24:01,373 --> 00:24:02,775
Go ahead, go ahead, come on.

570
00:24:02,775 --> 00:24:03,810
- Come on, Crow, come on.

571
00:24:03,810 --> 00:24:05,045
Don't put yourself through this.

572
00:24:05,045 --> 00:24:08,248
- No Mike, this is what
happens in the real world!

573
00:24:08,248 --> 00:24:09,983
Horrid lumps of discharge

574
00:24:09,983 --> 00:24:12,685
destroy beautiful innocent
robots with impunity.

575
00:24:12,685 --> 00:24:14,553
Laughing all the way.

576
00:24:14,553 --> 00:24:15,388
- You guys, you guys, calm down.

577
00:24:15,388 --> 00:24:16,890
I tell you what, I'll make a note to Pearl

578
00:24:16,890 --> 00:24:20,093
to go easy on the Gumby shorts, alright?

579
00:24:20,093 --> 00:24:21,227
- Thank you, Mike.

580
00:24:21,227 --> 00:24:24,965
- Dear Pearl, please go
easy on the Gumby shorts.

581
00:24:24,965 --> 00:24:26,767
- Oh, can you get some
dizzy grizzlies there, Mike?

582
00:24:26,767 --> 00:24:29,602
We need 'em for the
trauma we're experiencing.

583
00:24:29,602 --> 00:24:32,672
- Get dizzy grizzlies for trauma they're--

584
00:24:32,672 --> 00:24:34,307
We'll be right back.

585
00:24:34,307 --> 00:24:35,775
(crying)

586
00:24:35,775 --> 00:24:37,077
Come on, come on, soon you'll
be munching dizzy grizzlies.

587
00:24:37,077 --> 00:24:37,910
Calm down.

588
00:24:38,912 --> 00:24:41,515
(upbeat music)

589
00:24:49,688 --> 00:24:50,523
- [Mike] Well there he is, you know,

590
00:24:50,523 --> 00:24:53,025
have people wised up to
hiring guys like this?

591
00:24:53,025 --> 00:24:55,495
- Come on, Mike, how is
he any different than you?

592
00:24:55,495 --> 00:24:56,328
Really.

593
00:24:59,065 --> 00:25:01,233
- [Tom] I'll just grab
a goldfish for lunch.

594
00:25:05,805 --> 00:25:08,442
- [Mike] Ah, nothing like
a bath to perk me right up.

595
00:25:12,412 --> 00:25:14,680
- Well, I'd better go rotate the hostages.

596
00:25:19,652 --> 00:25:21,922
- [Tom] That's all gunk
that sloughed off Mickey.

597
00:25:24,157 --> 00:25:25,292
- Alright, that's done.

598
00:25:26,458 --> 00:25:28,662
- [Ms. Snow] Oh stop, please, be careful!

599
00:25:28,662 --> 00:25:31,230
(mumbling) darling, you'll break your leg.

600
00:25:31,230 --> 00:25:32,265
- [Eric] You're doing great, Reverend.

601
00:25:32,265 --> 00:25:33,098
- Don't worry.

602
00:25:33,098 --> 00:25:33,933
- Good heavens.

603
00:25:33,933 --> 00:25:35,868
Honestly, the two of them
are just like children.

604
00:25:35,868 --> 00:25:37,537
(laughing)
- Ah, it doesn't look scary.

605
00:25:37,537 --> 00:25:39,105
- Children love to hang drapes.

606
00:25:39,105 --> 00:25:41,107
- I hope you have more luck in
getting your husband to mind

607
00:25:41,107 --> 00:25:42,375
than I've had with mine.

608
00:25:42,375 --> 00:25:43,843
- You know, you've got to admit,

609
00:25:43,843 --> 00:25:46,112
it does make the room look better.

610
00:25:46,112 --> 00:25:46,980
- Very well (mumbling).

611
00:25:46,980 --> 00:25:49,348
For penance, you can
come help me with dinner.

612
00:25:49,348 --> 00:25:50,417
Now come on.

613
00:25:50,417 --> 00:25:51,952
Ed, would you keep Jenni company?

614
00:25:51,952 --> 00:25:53,085
- Yes dear.

615
00:25:53,085 --> 00:25:55,322
- [Mike] My wife and I
have an understanding.

616
00:25:58,292 --> 00:26:00,627
- Oh man, it's like a buffet of victims.

617
00:26:00,627 --> 00:26:02,262
I don't even know where to start.

618
00:26:05,698 --> 00:26:07,767
- [Tom] Van Gogh's Howdy!

619
00:26:09,702 --> 00:26:11,103
- No, Marion, no.

620
00:26:12,738 --> 00:26:13,840
Send them away.

621
00:26:13,840 --> 00:26:15,975
- [Mike] Exit stage left.

622
00:26:18,177 --> 00:26:19,845
(mumbling)

623
00:26:19,845 --> 00:26:22,682
- And I'm Zoroastrian and my
husband doesn't even know it.

624
00:26:22,682 --> 00:26:24,650
- She's so very nice, Eric.

625
00:26:24,650 --> 00:26:25,485
- Jenni.

626
00:26:25,485 --> 00:26:26,753
- Yes.

627
00:26:26,753 --> 00:26:28,422
- Isn't she wonderful?
- She's not at all like Marion

628
00:26:28,422 --> 00:26:29,422
and I think that's for the best.

629
00:26:29,422 --> 00:26:30,657
- [Tom] I went some place and I'm sad.

630
00:26:30,657 --> 00:26:32,825
- You know, so many men,
when they lose a wife,

631
00:26:32,825 --> 00:26:34,627
they try so hard to deny the loss

632
00:26:34,627 --> 00:26:37,263
and they marry someone
exactly like the first wife.

633
00:26:37,263 --> 00:26:39,198
Hardly seems fair using the living

634
00:26:39,198 --> 00:26:41,100
to bring back the dead, does it?

635
00:26:41,100 --> 00:26:42,968
- No, I suppose it doesn't.

636
00:26:42,968 --> 00:26:43,803
(laughing)

637
00:26:43,803 --> 00:26:44,837
- We make a prison for
ourselves out of the past.

638
00:26:44,837 --> 00:26:48,140
At least, our sentimental wished for past.

639
00:26:48,140 --> 00:26:49,275
- Ms. Snow.

640
00:26:49,275 --> 00:26:50,077
- [Ms. Snow] Yes dear?

641
00:26:50,077 --> 00:26:50,910
- [Mike] Shut up.

642
00:26:50,910 --> 00:26:52,778
- There's something I must
tell you and the reverend.

643
00:26:52,778 --> 00:26:55,782
- Well of course, Eric, what is it?

644
00:26:55,782 --> 00:26:57,850
- You see, Jenni has not
had a very happy past.

645
00:26:57,850 --> 00:26:58,685
- Oh?

646
00:26:58,685 --> 00:27:00,420
- And talking about it or about something

647
00:27:00,420 --> 00:27:03,088
that might strongly remind her of it,

648
00:27:03,088 --> 00:27:05,092
she's very impressionable.

649
00:27:05,092 --> 00:27:06,558
- Is there something wrong, Eric?

650
00:27:06,558 --> 00:27:07,960
- No, not really.

651
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:09,295
You see.

652
00:27:09,295 --> 00:27:10,130
- I bi-locate.

653
00:27:10,130 --> 00:27:12,532
- She lost her parents many years ago

654
00:27:12,532 --> 00:27:15,502
in a very tragic way and talking

655
00:27:15,502 --> 00:27:17,503
about unhappy pasts only,

656
00:27:18,338 --> 00:27:20,107
she's very impressionable.

657
00:27:20,107 --> 00:27:22,042
See, I want her to be happy, Ms. Snow.

658
00:27:22,042 --> 00:27:24,477
- Of course you do and so do we all.

659
00:27:24,477 --> 00:27:26,645
Now how did she lose them?

660
00:27:26,645 --> 00:27:27,580
- [Tom] She set them on top of the car

661
00:27:27,580 --> 00:27:28,448
and then drove off.

662
00:27:28,448 --> 00:27:30,050
- Well I'm not prying dear,

663
00:27:30,050 --> 00:27:31,852
it's just that Mr. Snow
and I can help better

664
00:27:31,852 --> 00:27:33,553
if we know something about it.

665
00:27:33,553 --> 00:27:35,688
- They drowned in an accident.

666
00:27:36,522 --> 00:27:37,523
- [Mike] Hi Debbie.

667
00:27:37,523 --> 00:27:38,825
- Jenni saw it all.

668
00:27:40,093 --> 00:27:41,527
- Thank you for the hand grenade.

669
00:27:41,527 --> 00:27:42,562
- Who's Mr. Mauer?

670
00:27:43,430 --> 00:27:44,263
- Mr. Mauer?

671
00:27:44,263 --> 00:27:46,265
Why, he's a lawyer in town.

672
00:27:46,265 --> 00:27:47,100
- Why?

673
00:27:47,100 --> 00:27:47,933
- I thought no one knew we were coming.

674
00:27:47,933 --> 00:27:49,935
You said you heard from Mr. Mauer.

675
00:27:49,935 --> 00:27:51,203
- Well Eric wrote him.

676
00:27:51,203 --> 00:27:52,438
- Why?

677
00:27:52,438 --> 00:27:54,540
- He takes care of the
estate or what's left of it.

678
00:27:54,540 --> 00:27:56,208
- Oh, that's right. Eric
has to see him tomorrow.

679
00:27:56,208 --> 00:27:57,410
- [Mike] Why, why?

680
00:27:57,410 --> 00:27:59,045
- Well Eric's co-executor of the state

681
00:27:59,045 --> 00:28:00,747
along with Mr. Mauer.

682
00:28:00,747 --> 00:28:02,548
You see, Marion's death was so sudden

683
00:28:02,548 --> 00:28:04,117
that, well, all that's was left to Eric

684
00:28:04,117 --> 00:28:06,285
was the house and these grounds.

685
00:28:07,120 --> 00:28:08,655
Mr. Mauer told me that Eric

686
00:28:08,655 --> 00:28:11,290
had found someone very sweet and very kind

687
00:28:11,290 --> 00:28:13,960
and with whom he was very much in love.

688
00:28:13,960 --> 00:28:15,528
He didn't say enough.

689
00:28:15,528 --> 00:28:17,730
- [Tom] Unfortunately
that all fell through.

690
00:28:20,333 --> 00:28:21,935
- [Mike] I still got it.

691
00:28:21,935 --> 00:28:24,003
- How did Marion die?

692
00:28:24,003 --> 00:28:24,970
- Didn't Eric tell you?

693
00:28:24,970 --> 00:28:27,107
- He sat on her head.

694
00:28:27,107 --> 00:28:29,508
- I think the subject's
rather painful to him.

695
00:28:29,508 --> 00:28:30,743
Don't like to make him talk about it.

696
00:28:30,743 --> 00:28:33,547
- [Tom] Let's just say she
was riddled with bullets.

697
00:28:33,547 --> 00:28:34,913
- Would you mind telling me?

698
00:28:34,913 --> 00:28:37,183
I'd like to know.

699
00:28:37,183 --> 00:28:38,718
- It was a rainy day.

700
00:28:38,718 --> 00:28:40,387
- [Mike] And he shot her.

701
00:28:40,387 --> 00:28:41,520
- She and Mickey had been working up there

702
00:28:41,520 --> 00:28:43,557
in the greenhouse.

703
00:28:43,557 --> 00:28:46,058
She left him to go back to
the house for a few minutes.

704
00:28:46,058 --> 00:28:47,660
- Then a lion jumped out and shot her.

705
00:28:47,660 --> 00:28:49,295
At least that's what Eric said.

706
00:28:49,295 --> 00:28:50,730
- The way we pieced it together

707
00:28:50,730 --> 00:28:52,465
after the accident is that--

708
00:28:52,465 --> 00:28:53,533
- [Tom] She got shot.

709
00:28:53,533 --> 00:28:55,402
- While she was coming down this path,

710
00:28:56,402 --> 00:28:58,403
apparently it began to rain very hard.

711
00:28:58,403 --> 00:28:59,572
- [Tom] Then she got dead.

712
00:28:59,572 --> 00:29:01,107
- She must have run along here.

713
00:29:02,875 --> 00:29:04,943
We don't know of course
what happened then.

714
00:29:04,943 --> 00:29:06,412
- [Mike] We should have called the police.

715
00:29:06,412 --> 00:29:08,080
- Perhaps she slipped on a leaf.

716
00:29:09,482 --> 00:29:11,250
The base of her skull was smashed.

717
00:29:12,718 --> 00:29:13,920
- Anyway, how are you?

718
00:29:16,923 --> 00:29:18,825
- It was thought that she hit her head

719
00:29:18,825 --> 00:29:20,127
on the edge of this cement wall

720
00:29:20,127 --> 00:29:23,228
where we're sitting and she fell in there.

721
00:29:24,430 --> 00:29:26,032
She died in the water.

722
00:29:26,032 --> 00:29:28,968
That's where Eric found
her 10 minutes later.

723
00:29:28,968 --> 00:29:31,070
- [Tom] Dave Pruner searches for his ball.

724
00:29:36,175 --> 00:29:37,010
- Marion.

725
00:29:39,178 --> 00:29:40,180
Marion.

726
00:29:40,180 --> 00:29:44,950
You said, you said Mickey, wait here.

727
00:29:44,950 --> 00:29:45,785
(laughing)

728
00:29:45,785 --> 00:29:49,655
I'm going down to the
house for just a minute.

729
00:29:51,090 --> 00:29:53,458
Wait here, Mickey, you said.

730
00:29:53,458 --> 00:29:54,627
And then you went away in the rain.

731
00:29:54,627 --> 00:29:56,128
- [Mike] Oh, he's growing pot.

732
00:29:57,163 --> 00:29:59,498
- And you didn't come back to play.

733
00:29:59,498 --> 00:30:01,167
Marion, why did you--

734
00:30:01,167 --> 00:30:02,735
- Get a box!

735
00:30:02,735 --> 00:30:05,705
(whimpering)

736
00:30:05,705 --> 00:30:07,140
- And don't forget that you and Eric

737
00:30:07,140 --> 00:30:09,242
are coming for lunch
the day after tomorrow.

738
00:30:09,242 --> 00:30:10,377
- We'll do better than that.

739
00:30:10,377 --> 00:30:12,378
We'll come to church on Sunday as well.

740
00:30:12,378 --> 00:30:13,412
(laughing)

741
00:30:13,412 --> 00:30:16,348
- Getting Eric to church
is like moving a mountain.

742
00:30:16,348 --> 00:30:17,617
- We'll come.

743
00:30:17,617 --> 00:30:18,785
- [Reverend Snow] Come along
my dear, it's getting late.

744
00:30:18,785 --> 00:30:20,320
- [Mike] Yes, you, hot stuff.

745
00:30:20,320 --> 00:30:22,322
- Eric, thank you very much

746
00:30:22,322 --> 00:30:24,590
for bringing Jenni into our lives.

747
00:30:24,590 --> 00:30:26,525
- [Eric] Thank you for the dinner.

748
00:30:26,525 --> 00:30:28,427
- [Ms. Snow] It was a
pleasure meeting you.

749
00:30:28,427 --> 00:30:29,462
Good night.

750
00:30:29,462 --> 00:30:30,797
- [Reverend Snow] Good night.

751
00:30:30,797 --> 00:30:32,698
- [Tom] What the hell was that?

752
00:30:32,698 --> 00:30:34,733
- Did you know that Jenni's very wealthy?

753
00:30:34,733 --> 00:30:36,668
- Why yes, Mr. Mauer
told me in town today.

754
00:30:36,668 --> 00:30:38,270
- She's not at all like Marion, you know.

755
00:30:38,270 --> 00:30:39,705
She's so gentle and timid

756
00:30:39,705 --> 00:30:42,342
as if she were afraid of something.

757
00:30:42,342 --> 00:30:44,877
- I did have a gun in her
ribs during dinner, but--

758
00:30:46,112 --> 00:30:47,980
- I knew you'd like my friends, dear.

759
00:30:49,715 --> 00:30:51,583
What's this, huh?

760
00:30:52,652 --> 00:30:54,420
- Just happy, that's all.

761
00:30:54,420 --> 00:30:55,688
- Blech.

762
00:30:55,688 --> 00:30:56,990
- So happy.

763
00:30:56,990 --> 00:30:57,823
- Come on.

764
00:31:01,660 --> 00:31:03,362
- [Tom] Hey, nice pad he's got there.

765
00:31:03,362 --> 00:31:04,197
- Shut up.

766
00:31:06,900 --> 00:31:08,735
- [Mike] I'm gonna start my own pond.

767
00:31:11,170 --> 00:31:13,773
(upbeat music)

768
00:31:17,777 --> 00:31:21,280
(foreboding music)

769
00:31:21,280 --> 00:31:23,482
- [Mike] Ah, I could read
Paul Reiser's autobiography

770
00:31:23,482 --> 00:31:25,552
over and over again.
- [Eric] How are the cots?

771
00:31:25,552 --> 00:31:26,852
- [Tom] I have a headache.

772
00:31:28,020 --> 00:31:28,855
- Fine.

773
00:31:28,855 --> 00:31:30,490
- It was either the clown wallpaper

774
00:31:30,490 --> 00:31:32,858
or the flat gray paint.

775
00:31:32,858 --> 00:31:34,060
- What are you reading?

776
00:31:34,060 --> 00:31:36,028
- [Mike] Avoiding the
Old Goat by Ann Landers.

777
00:31:36,028 --> 00:31:37,563
- Something Ms. Snow sent over.

778
00:31:39,298 --> 00:31:40,165
- Interesting.

779
00:31:40,165 --> 00:31:41,400
- It was.

780
00:31:43,535 --> 00:31:44,770
- [Jenni] It's called Beast in the Jungle.

781
00:31:44,770 --> 00:31:47,273
- [Tom] It's about what
to expect on my honeymoon.

782
00:31:49,542 --> 00:31:50,377
- It's all about a man

783
00:31:50,377 --> 00:31:54,013
who waited all of his
life for something great

784
00:31:54,013 --> 00:31:55,782
and wonderful to happen to him.

785
